\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Safety in Reasoning Models.}
Reasoning models introduce a qualitatively new failure mode: the model's own chain of thought transitions from safe deliberation to harmful generation without adversarial input~\cite{wang2025lrmsafety}. Self-jailbreak has been documented with step-level annotations~\cite{harmthoughts,selfjailbreak}; H-CoT~\cite{hcot2025} shows reasoning chains can be externally hijacked; unfaithful reasoning~\cite{chen2025unfaithful,turpin2023language} motivates hidden-state monitoring. Safety-aligned models maintain refusal intentions during thinking but experience sharp drops at output generation~\cite{yin2025refusal_cliff,hagendorff2026lrm_jailbreak,hu2026refusal_dynamics,ahrend2026safer_traces}. CoT monitoring detects reward hacking but is fragile under optimization pressure~\cite{baker2025monitoring,korbak2025cot_monitorability}; GuardReasoner~\cite{xiang2025guardreasoner} trains a dedicated reasoning guard; \citet{schulz2025roadmap} formalizes safety-case deployment. Mitigation strategies include early reasoning steering~\cite{safethink}, entropy-triggered reminders~\cite{saferemind}, and guardrail insertion~\cite{mao2025selfjailbreak_reasoning}; none conducts a systematic modality comparison for commitment detection.

\paragraph{Hidden-State Safety Detection.}
Internal activations encode safety-relevant information: truthfulness~\cite{azaria2023internal}, layer-distributed alignment signals~\cite{zhou2024alignment}, shallow alignment~\cite{qi2025shallow_alignment}, refusal directions~\cite{arditi2024refusal,joad2026refusal_multi}, activation steering~\cite{turner2023activation,li2023iti}, separate harmfulness/refusal encodings~\cite{zhao2025harmfulness,wollschlager2025geometry}, and scalable feature extraction~\cite{templeton2024scaling}. Probing studies confirm safety states are linearly accessible~\cite{zhao2026safeprobing,chia2025probing_subspaces,kadali2025internal}. Monitoring systems---TrajGuard~\cite{trajguard}, SafeSwitch~\cite{safeswitch}, SIREN~\cite{siren}, SafeDream~\cite{safedream2026}, HiddenDetect~\cite{jiang2025hiddendetect}, SafeInt~\cite{safeint2025}, RCS~\cite{rcs2026}, and hidden-state forensics~\cite{zhou2025ghost}---assume external input manipulation and operate at the token level; the self-jailbreak setting differs because transitions are internal and span multiple reasoning steps. Concurrently, \citet{chan2025probing} show final-layer linear probes predict alignment outcomes with strong F1, but their single-layer, single-metric evaluation does not examine whether different layers yield different conclusions---the central question of our work (\cref{sec:layer-results}). We reproduce TrajGuard (\cref{sec:negative}) and find its geometric approach fails on minor PCA components.\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Probing Methodology and Layer Selection.}
Linear probing spans layer-wise classifiers~\cite{alain2017understanding}, sentence probing~\cite{conneau2018probing}, and surveys~\cite{belinkov2019analysis,belinkov2022probing,rogers2020primer}; causal abstraction~\cite{geiger2023causal} grounds use of internal representations. Control tasks~\cite{hewitt2019control} distinguish structure from memorization (adopted in \cref{app:control}); \citet{levinstein2024liedetector} raise concerns about spurious correlations that our controls address. Information-theoretic and causal formulations address probe complexity~\cite{voita2020information,pimentel2020information,ravfogel2020null,ravfogel2022rlace,belrose2023leace,elazar2021amnesic}.\footnote{The Tuned Lens~\cite{belrose2023tunedlens} assumes next-token prediction, misaligned with binary CP classification.} Non-uniform layer-wise information distribution~\cite{tenney2019bert,jawahar2019bert,marks2024geometry} and cross-layer disagreement~\cite{kaya2019shallow,zhou2020bert} are well established; we repurpose these for safety probe layer selection (\cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}). Our contribution is that the known layer-wise gradient interacts with metric choice to produce systematic selection bias. \citet{boxo2025linear_probes} show probes can rely on textual evidence; our analysis provides a complementary geometric mechanism at shallow layers. Concurrent works confirm layer sensitivity in related settings: influence estimation~\cite{vitel2025layer}, reasoning dynamics monitoring~\cite{chrabaszcz2026monitoring}, hallucination detection~\cite{alvarez2026transport_geometry}, truth encoding trajectories~\cite{damirchi2026truth}, selective probes~\cite{meyoyan2026bertology}, and multi-layer ensembling~\cite{pais2026linear_probe_scaling}.\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Alternative Detection Approaches.}
BOCPD~\cite{adams2007bayesian} detects abrupt distributional shifts; CCS~\cite{burns2023discovering} discovers latent truth directions; RepE~\cite{zou2023representation} identifies concept directions. We adapt all three to temporal commitment detection (\cref{sec:negative}): BOCPD/CUSUM fail on gradual transitions, CCS misaligns temporally, and RepE is model-dependent (BA 0.756 vs.\ 0.559). These failures motivate supervised probes with principled layer selection.\looseness=-1

